{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 405 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "2ed12d1a08ea1025d4e9c8e993c44f99856187640fd72d978b506fe22b5dfe32" :edb/db-before-t 404 :edb/db-after-t 405 :edb/tx-data [[13194139533717 50 #inst "2026-10-04T18:45:50.978Z" 13194139533717 true] [17592186054518 1020 #edb/ref 17592186248187 13194139533717 true] [17592186054662 1020 #edb/ref 17592186248184 13194139533717 true] [17592186059606 1020 #edb/ref 17592186248177 13194139533717 true] [17592186059717 1020 #edb/ref 17592186248170 13194139533717 true] [17592186059769 1020 #edb/ref 17592186248190 13194139533717 true] [17592186068682 1020 #edb/ref 17592186248197 13194139533717 true] [17592186073655 1020 #edb/ref 17592186248204 13194139533717 true] [17592186078378 1020 #edb/ref 17592186248163 13194139533717 true] [17592186248163 1017 #edb/ref 17592186248164 13194139533717 true] [17592186248163 1089 "q-112047" 13194139533717 true] [17592186248163 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533717 true] [17592186248163 1100 "Factor the expression ${g}^{4}-1\\,296{h}^{4}.$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the fact that $1\\,296={36}^{2}.$*" 13194139533717 true] [17592186248163 1113 #uuid "94568326-4add-523a-9970-740075840c8b" 13194139533717 true] [17592186248163 1138 false 13194139533717 true] [17592186248163 1145 "The difference of squares formula is given by\n\n$$\n{a}^{2}-{b}^{2}=(a+b)(a-b)\n$$\n\nNotice that our expression can be written as a difference of squares:\n\n$$\n{g}^{4}-1\\,296{h}^{4}={({g}^{2})}^{2}-{(36{h}^{2})}^{2}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor our expression using the difference of squares formula:\n\n$$\n{({g}^{2})}^{2}-{(36{h}^{2})}^{2}=({g}^{2}+36{h}^{2})({g}^{2}-36{h}^{2})\n$$\n\nFinally, we notice that the expression in the second set of parentheses is also a difference of squares:\n\n$$\n{g}^{2}-36{h}^{2}={g}^{2}-(6h)^{2}\n$$\n\n Therefore, the expression can be factored further, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({g}^{2}+36{h}^{2})({g}^{2}-36{h}^{2}) & =({g}^{2}+36{h}^{2})(g+6h)(g-6h)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533717 true] [17592186248164 1000 #edb/ref 17592186248165 13194139533717 true] [17592186248164 1000 #edb/ref 17592186248166 13194139533717 true] [17592186248164 1000 #edb/ref 17592186248167 13194139533717 true] [17592186248164 1000 #edb/ref 17592186248168 13194139533717 true] [17592186248164 1000 #edb/ref 17592186248169 13194139533717 true] [17592186248164 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533717 true] [17592186248164 1041 "selection" 13194139533717 true] [17592186248164 1104 #edb/ref 17592186248165 13194139533717 true] [17592186248164 1110 #uuid "9d97dd84-6ec5-5e6d-b590-bb936884603d" 13194139533717 true] [17592186248165 1034 "({g}^{2}+36{h}^{2})(g+6h)(g-6h)" 13194139533717 true] [17592186248165 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533717 true] [17592186248165 1111 #uuid "8efbeae1-3516-5eb3-b963-75537441d57f" 13194139533717 true] [17592186248166 1034 "(g+36h)(g+6h)(g-6h)" 13194139533717 true] [17592186248166 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533717 true] [17592186248166 1111 #uuid "7218c5d1-3ab4-5c51-afd1-0093e5cde9c4" 13194139533717 true] [17592186248167 1034 "({g}^{2}+6{h}^{2})(g+3h)(g-3h)" 13194139533717 true] [17592186248167 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533717 true] [17592186248167 1111 #uuid "56ea3847-abda-549a-80c9-5979409836b9" 13194139533717 true] [17592186248168 1034 "(g-6h)^{4}" 13194139533717 true] [17592186248168 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533717 true] [17592186248168 1111 #uuid "51bad81e-477f-5f02-9dd2-db1434c0bd5f" 13194139533717 true] [17592186248169 1034 "({g}^{2}+36{h}^{2})(g-6h)^{2}" 13194139533717 true] [17592186248169 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533717 true] [17592186248169 1111 #uuid "88a60872-fafd-50e3-a265-6a36b233d89a" 13194139533717 true] [17592186248170 1017 #edb/ref 17592186248171 13194139533717 true] [17592186248170 1089 "q-122082" 13194139533717 true] [17592186248170 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533717 true] [17592186248170 1100 "David wants to play with one of his favorite toys. He has $3$ action figures, $8$ cars, $2$ building blocks sets, and $1$ electronic device. How many choices of toys does David have?" 13194139533717 true] [17592186248170 1113 #uuid "943b9eea-882f-5331-8325-3e39142318ab" 13194139533717 true] [17592186248170 1138 false 13194139533717 true] [17592186248170 1145 "In order to find the total number of choices David has, we can use the **rule of sum**:\n\n$$\n3+8+2+1=14\n$$\n\nTherefore, David has a choice of $14$ toys." 13194139533717 true] [17592186248171 1000 #edb/ref 17592186248172 13194139533717 true] [17592186248171 1000 #edb/ref 17592186248173 13194139533717 true] [17592186248171 1000 #edb/ref 17592186248174 13194139533717 true] [17592186248171 1000 #edb/ref 17592186248175 13194139533717 true] [17592186248171 1000 #edb/ref 17592186248176 13194139533717 true] [17592186248171 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533717 true] [17592186248171 1041 "selection" 13194139533717 true] [17592186248171 1104 #edb/ref 17592186248174 13194139533717 true] [17592186248171 1110 #uuid "c5904bd9-652c-5937-aac6-a38a6a23ec58" 13194139533717 true] [17592186248172 1034 "13" 13194139533717 true] [17592186248172 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533717 true] [17592186248172 1111 #uuid "251d5a53-c2f4-5a75-9b30-ea3839d19404" 13194139533717 true] [17592186248173 1034 "11" 13194139533717 true] [17592186248173 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533717 true] [17592186248173 1111 #uuid "75ecc73e-3608-57fc-a752-90f7a4471c2f" 13194139533717 true] [17592186248174 1034 "14" 13194139533717 true] [17592186248174 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533717 true] [17592186248174 1111 #uuid "0eaa0324-b789-5848-a11f-6c66434977bf" 13194139533717 true] [17592186248175 1034 "6" 13194139533717 true] [17592186248175 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533717 true] [17592186248175 1111 #uuid "4a430658-97a0-54f2-9275-e33ab24db993" 13194139533717 true] [17592186248176 1034 "4" 13194139533717 true] [17592186248176 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533717 true] [17592186248176 1111 #uuid "636cbc9c-ac02-5f79-8e3e-26e8bc2f0091" 13194139533717 true] [17592186248177 1017 #edb/ref 17592186248178 13194139533717 true] [17592186248177 1089 "q-124716" 13194139533717 true] [17592186248177 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533717 true] [17592186248177 1100 "The angle $\\theta =-\\frac{43\\pi }{12}$ is represented in the coordinate plane in the usual way. In which quadrant does it lie?" 13194139533717 true] [17592186248177 1113 #uuid "d0e55934-d01a-5c52-b15c-95be9b2ee854" 13194139533717 true] [17592186248177 1138 false 13194139533717 true] [17592186248177 1145 "First, we find an angle that's coterminal with $-\\frac{43\\pi }{12}$ by *adding* integer multiples of $2\\pi $ until we get an angle that lies in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$\n\n- $-\\frac{43\\pi }{12}+2\\pi =-\\frac{19\\pi }{12}$\n\n- $-\\frac{43\\pi }{12}+2\\cdot (2\\pi )=\\frac{5\\pi }{12}\\,✓$\n\nSo the angle $-\\frac{43\\pi }{12}$ is coterminal with $\\frac{5\\pi }{12}.$ Let's draw this coterminal angle in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13938136/assets/98324683d6b71e6026341cd70e2c84acb35bb117bd0bb41b627121b3b4f17a79.png)\n\nWe see that $\\frac{5\\pi }{12}$ lies in quadrant I. Therefore, $-\\frac{43\\pi }{12}$ also lies in this quadrant." 13194139533717 true] [17592186248178 1000 #edb/ref 17592186248179 13194139533717 true] [17592186248178 1000 #edb/ref 17592186248180 13194139533717 true] [17592186248178 1000 #edb/ref 17592186248181 13194139533717 true] [17592186248178 1000 #edb/ref 17592186248182 13194139533717 true] [17592186248178 1000 #edb/ref 17592186248183 13194139533717 true] [17592186248178 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533717 true] [17592186248178 1041 "selection" 13194139533717 true] [17592186248178 1104 #edb/ref 17592186248183 13194139533717 true] [17592186248178 1110 #uuid "86beff0f-f2c3-575e-9f2f-07c8a9bfb612" 13194139533717 true] [17592186248179 1034 "Between quadrants I and II" 13194139533717 true] [17592186248179 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533717 true] [17592186248179 1111 #uuid "d59d6a9b-bcb6-50f1-a667-50bf186d1265" 13194139533717 true] [17592186248180 1034 "II" 13194139533717 true] [17592186248180 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533717 true] [17592186248180 1111 #uuid "14574a40-341f-5a04-94e3-4dcb7cde822d" 13194139533717 true] [17592186248181 1034 "III" 13194139533717 true] [17592186248181 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533717 true] [17592186248181 1111 #uuid "b11893c0-0c0b-5125-a6c5-dd0e4c72ee69" 13194139533717 true] [17592186248182 1034 "IV" 13194139533717 true] [17592186248182 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533717 true] [17592186248182 1111 #uuid "cff0057b-9658-5442-a7fc-64e229d19e05" 13194139533717 true] [17592186248183 1034 "I" 13194139533717 true] [17592186248183 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533717 true] [17592186248183 1111 #uuid "8e75c66f-e505-5275-9fd3-17257d77e79a" 13194139533717 true] [17592186248184 1017 #edb/ref 17592186248185 13194139533717 true] [17592186248184 1089 "q-278326" 13194139533717 true] [17592186248184 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533717 true] [17592186248184 1100 "The multiplicity of the root $x=-10$ in the polynomial $p(x)={x}^{3}+30{x}^{2}+300x+1000$ is{{field-1}}." 13194139533717 true] [17592186248184 1113 #uuid "c7e641bf-c9dd-5010-81cc-3e5834590a0f" 13194139533717 true] [17592186248184 1138 false 13194139533717 true] [17592186248184 1145 "Since $x=-10$ is a root of $p(x),$ the factor theorem tells us that $(x+10)$ is a factor of $p(x).$\n\nBecause $(x+10)$ is a factor of $p(x),$ we know that $(x+10)$ divides $p(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $p(x),$ we can divide it by $(x+10)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-10$ | $1$ | $30$ | $300$ | $1000$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $-10$ | $-200$ | $-1000$ |\n|  | $1$ | $20$ | $100$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{p(x)}{x+10}={x}^{2}+20x+100,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\np(x)=(x+10)({x}^{2}+20x+100).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(x) & =(x+10)(x+10)^{2} \\\\  & =(x+10)^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe factor $(x+10)$ has an exponent of $3$ in the factored form of $p(x).$ So the root $x=-10$ has multiplicity $3.$" 13194139533717 true] [17592186248185 1000 #edb/ref 17592186248186 13194139533717 true] [17592186248185 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533717 true] [17592186248185 1041 "field-1" 13194139533717 true] [17592186248185 1104 #edb/ref 17592186248186 13194139533717 true] [17592186248185 1110 #uuid "2922d52a-1275-5801-9173-26241151ceb6" 13194139533717 true] [17592186248186 1034 "3" 13194139533717 true] [17592186248186 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533717 true] [17592186248186 1111 #uuid "158601eb-5d2c-56be-9640-a63182532af1" 13194139533717 true] [17592186248187 1017 #edb/ref 17592186248188 13194139533717 true] [17592186248187 1089 "q-338426" 13194139533717 true] [17592186248187 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533717 true] [17592186248187 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13938136/assets/52c11d9ff3afeef74c579d4cb2c5448c9f5b77b7d6569535214bb67408276026.png)\n\nIf the figure above shows the graph of the functions $f(x)$ and $g(x),$ then\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,1}\\sqrt{\\frac{3f(x)-g(x)}{f(x)}}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533717 true] [17592186248187 1113 #uuid "3f202e8b-630d-50de-8a3d-0b543130e628" 13194139533717 true] [17592186248187 1138 false 13194139533717 true] [17592186248187 1145 "From the graph, we get that ${lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)=2$ and ${lim}_{x\\to \\,1}g(x)=4.$\n\nApplying the root and the quotient rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,1}\\sqrt{\\frac{3f(x)-g(x)}{f(x)}} & =\\sqrt{\\,{lim}_{x\\to \\,1}\\frac{3f(x)-g(x)}{f(x)}\\,} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\,\\frac{{lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(3f(x)-g(x))}{{lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)}\\,}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the limits of the numerator and denominator separately. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(3f(x)-g(x)) & =3(2)-4 \\\\  & =6-4 \\\\  & =2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)=2.\n$$\n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,1}\\sqrt{\\frac{3f(x)-g(x)}{f(x)}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{2}} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533717 true] [17592186248188 1000 #edb/ref 17592186248189 13194139533717 true] [17592186248188 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533717 true] [17592186248188 1041 "field-1" 13194139533717 true] [17592186248188 1104 #edb/ref 17592186248189 13194139533717 true] [17592186248188 1110 #uuid "201d794d-dae3-5b0d-bd63-0b6542fef142" 13194139533717 true] [17592186248189 1034 "1" 13194139533717 true] [17592186248189 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533717 true] [17592186248189 1111 #uuid "5182805d-fda4-5a06-96b7-0692777707b2" 13194139533717 true] [17592186248190 1017 #edb/ref 17592186248191 13194139533717 true] [17592186248190 1089 "q-64927" 13194139533717 true] [17592186248190 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533717 true] [17592186248190 1100 "$cos⁡(\\frac{11\\pi }{6})=$" 13194139533717 true] [17592186248190 1113 #uuid "926640fe-3830-5c44-85a6-d06f89a5ca73" 13194139533717 true] [17592186248190 1138 false 13194139533717 true] [17592186248190 1145 "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13938136/assets/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{11\\pi }{6}$ corresponds to the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},-\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\nSince the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $cos⁡(\\frac{11\\pi }{6})=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.$" 13194139533717 true] [17592186248191 1000 #edb/ref 17592186248192 13194139533717 true] [17592186248191 1000 #edb/ref 17592186248193 131941395